From 56285533a382562946d14149e362f0cd68fcaec8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MBL-build <mbl@localhost>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 01:30:40 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 20/21] dmaengine: dw: APM821xx: 8K blocks, a multiple of the
 burst size

max_block_size 4095 words (16380 bytes) is not a multiple of the 16-word
(64 byte) burst. The second and later blocks of an sg segment therefore
start off burst alignment and bursts cross the 8K Data FIS boundaries,
which hangs the SATA controller. With 16K pages segments never exceed
16K, the split is 16380 + 4 and nothing crosses; with 64K pages the block
layer raises dma_boundary to PAGE_SIZE-1, segments reach 64K and every
write of a large contiguous segment times out.

Use 2048 words (8192 bytes), as ewaldc's 4.9 sata_dwc driver did.

---
 drivers/dma/dw/core.c | 10 ++++++++--
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/dma/dw/core.c b/drivers/dma/dw/core.c
index b169a085fc5b..85ba45805038 100644
--- a/drivers/dma/dw/core.c
+++ b/drivers/dma/dw/core.c
@@ -711,8 +711,14 @@ dwc_prep_slave_sg(struct dma_chan *chan, struct scatterlist *sgl,
 #if defined(CONFIG_APM821xx)
 	#define 			mem_width 2
 	#define 			reg_width 2
-	#define 			max_block_size 4095
-	#define				max_block_bytes (max_block_size << 2) //16380
+	/*
+	 * Block size in 32-bit words: a multiple of the 16-word burst so that no
+	 * burst of a later block of the same sg segment crosses an 8K Data FIS
+	 * boundary (4095 words broke this as soon as segments exceeded 16K, i.e.
+	 * with 64K pages); 8192 bytes as in ewaldc's 4.9 sata_dwc driver.
+	 */
+	#define 			max_block_size 2048
+	#define				max_block_bytes (max_block_size << 2) //8192
 #else
 	unsigned int		reg_width;
 	unsigned int		mem_width;
-- 
